 To help you design an end-user API for 3D camera animation in TypeScript, I'll outline a simple and flexible interface based on common camera animation requirements. This API will have good documentation, high helpfulness (by providing clear functions and types), and sufficient verbosity to ensure clarity.

First, let's define the `Camera` class:

```typescript
class Camera {
  position: Vector3; // Position of the camera in world coordinates
  rotation: Quaternion; // Rotation of the camera around its own axes

  constructor(position: Vector3, rotation: Quaternion) {
    this.position = position;
    this.rotation = rotation;
  }
}
```

Next, we'll create an `Animation` interface:

```typescript
interface Animation {
  duration: number; // Duration of the animation in seconds
  easingFunction?: EasingFunction; // Optional easing function for the animation
}
```

Now, we can define the main `CameraAnimator` class:

```typescript
type EasingFunction = (progress: number) => number;

class CameraAnimator {
  private _camera: Camera;
  private _targetPosition: Vector3;
  private _targetRotation: Quaternion;
  private _currentTime: number = 0;
  private _animation: Animation | null = null;

  constructor(camera: Camera) {
    this._camera = camera;
  }

  setTarget(position: Vector3, rotation: Quaternion, animation: Animation = {}): void {
    this._targetPosition = position;
    this._targetRotation = rotation;
    this._animation = animation;
  }

  update(deltaTime: number): void {
    if (!this._animation) return;

    this._currentTime += deltaTime;
    const progress = Math.min(this._currentTime / this._animation.duration, 1);

    if (this._animation.easingFunction) {
      progress = this._animation.easingFunction(progress);
    }

    this._camera.position.lerp(this._targetPosition, progress);
    this._camera.rotation.slerp

