 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that uses regular expressions and string manipulation to switch the order of adjectives in a given sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  // Define regex pattern to match adjectives before nouns
  const adjectivePattern = /([\w']+ (\w+|j[a-z])[\s]*(\w+|j[a-z]))/g;

  let matches = [],
      match,
      newSentence = sentence;

  // Find all adjective-noun pairs in the sentence
  while ((match = adjectivePattern.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    matches.push(match);
  }

  // Reorder the adjectives for each pair
  for (let i = matches.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    const adj1 = matches[i][1],
         adj2 = matches[i][3];
    newSentence = newSentence.replace(
      new RegExp('\\b' + adj1 + ' ([^ ]+)', 'g'),
      ($1, noun) => adj2 + ' ' + $1
    );
  }

  return newSentence;
}

// Test the function
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
console.log(switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence)); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function first defines a regular expression pattern to find all adjective-noun pairs in the sentence. It then iterates through these pairs and reorders the adjectives by replacing the first adjective with the second one in the sentence using a regular expression replacement. Finally, it returns the modified sentence with the swapped adjectives.

