1. Initialize the GCM instance with a secret key and optional initialization vector (IV). Make sure that you have access to a secure source of random data to generate an IV if one is not provided by the implementation.

```java
SecretKey key = ...; // your 16 bytes or 32 bytes (for AES-128 or AES-256 respectively) secret key
byte[] iv = ...; // your 12 bytes IV, optionally generated using SecureRandom
GCM gcm = GCM.newInstance(key);
if (iv != null) {
    gcm.init(true, new IvParameterSpec(iv));
} else {
    gcm.init(true, key);
}
```

2. Update the Additional Authenticated Data (AAD) using the `updateAAD` method. This method is used when you want to provide some data that will be authenticated but not encrypted. In this step, you can call this method multiple times to add more AAD.

```java
byte[] aad = "Some AAD data".getBytes("UTF-8"); // 16 bytes or less
gcm.updateAAD(aad);
```

3. Perform encryption and authentication on the plaintext message. First, clear any pending data from the cipher by calling the `reset()` method, then update the cipher with the message and perform the encryption. The cipher will automatically compute the authenticator tag during encryption.

